    {
      "id": "p0113-b002",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Traditionally, the Japanese personal pronoun oira suggests that the speaker is from a rural or uncultured area. Oira is slowly fading from everyday use, however, and is used far less commonly than pronouns like watashi, boku, and ore.",
        "raw": "Traditionally, the Japanese personal pronoun oira suggests that the\nspeaker is from a rural or uncultured area. Oira is slowly fading from\neveryday use, however, and is used far less commonly than pronouns like\nwatashi, boku, and ore.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.11777852,
          0.11881429,
          0.67549354,
          0.18036429
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.11573676,
          0.1181,
          0.67753529,
          0.18143571
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          2,
          3,
          4,
          5
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "传统上，日语人称代词 oira（我）暗示说话者来自乡下或文化不发达的地方。不过，oira 正逐渐淡出日常用语，远不如 watashi、boku、ore 这些表示“我”的代词常用。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": [
